folks I don't need to tell you how good  God of War Ragnarok looks you've seen  the trailers you've seen the game play  what I'm here to tell you is how to  truly appreciate ragnarok's visuals and  all its glorious and godly detail the  secret play in immersive mode that's  right playing God of War without a HUD  might seem a bit ridiculous but Ragnarok  like 2018's God of War was designed with  immersive mode in mind at least to some  extent it's more challenging but if  you're a glutton for punishment Like  Yours Truly then it might just be the  best way to play so let's start at the  beginning you can turn on immersive mode  from the HUD settings in the pause menu  there are actually a few options here  default immersive and hidden the fall  gives you the standard HUD immersive  turns off the HUD but with the swipe of  a touchpad critical information will  populate the screen hidden removes the  HUD altogether if you're feeling really  confident you can hide the HUD entirely  however in my experience I prefer to  have it easily accessible just in case  send you to check the compass  occasionally you may also want to check  on a boss's health bar because some  battles can be quite long so what  information are you missing out on by  turning on immersive mode well you can't  see kratos's health bar enemy health  bars enemy levels the enemy indicator  kratos's rage meter the compass  companion information including atreus's  Arrow count and text pop-ups in theory  immersive mode removes all the  extraneous HUD elements so you can focus  on the oh so brutal action in front of  you  oh  not seeing kratos's health bar isn't as  much of a problem as it may seem when  that red vignette starts to appear your  health is low either make some distance  between you and your enemies or look for  some green crystals to heal up if you've  been keeping Kratos well equipped you  might be able to take a few lighter hits  when that red vignette appears but I  wouldn't take too many chances no enemy  health bars are a little bit tougher to  get used to though especially when  fighting tankier enemies for most  standard enemies there aren't any tells  that a foe is getting weak this means  you have to hack at them until the red  finisher ring pops up or they just die  speaking of finishers you also can't see  an enemy stagger meter this means that  if you want to use a finisher you need  to focus your aggression on a specific  enemy until you see that red ring just  be ready to click the right stick when  that red ring appears without the  Stagger meter it can sometimes appear  out of nowhere especially for boss  fights the ring does linger for a bit  however if you're too caught up in your  ax swinging you might kill the enemy too  quickly  thankfully even in immersive mode you  can still see the attack Rings tied to  specific enemy moves the red circle  still means it's unblockable yellow  means you'll get staggered if you don't  counter and blue means you can Shield  break an enemy responding to these cues  properly is crucial in immersive mode  this is the only non-diegetic  information immersive mode gives you so  make sure to take advantage of these  moments whenever possible  now one of the coolest things about  immersive mode is that because you don't  have an enemy indicator watching your  back you need to listen to your  companions when enemies approach from  behind this happened in 2018 as well but  it's far more reliable in Ragnarok  atreus or mamir will accurately make  callouts in basically every combat  scenario  it makes sense given that mimir is  basically the eyes in the back of  kratos's head or but I guess even if  immersive mode doesn't sound like your  thing I'd still recommend turning off  the enemy indicator relying on your  companions to have your back rather than  a little arrow gives your friends more  agency and makes the experience a little  more authentic not being able to see  kratos's rage meter also makes immersive  mode a tad more tricky once again  there's no visual indicator when  kratos's rage is full so it's impossible  to know when you should use it I  typically save rage for a tricky fight  or a boss and in my experience this has  worked pretty well I'm definitely not as  efficient with my rage as I would be if  I played with the default HUD but you  can't have it all  oh  bro  as I mentioned before the compass is the  main reason why I don't recommend  turning off the HUD altogether while I  wouldn't consider God of War Ragnarok an  open world game there are still a lot of  open areas that you might get lost in  being able to mark something on the map  and swipe a touchpad to see the compass  saves a ton of time I guess if you  really want to play with the HUD hidden  you could reference your map whenever  you get lost but continually flipping  back and forth got pretty distracting  for me especially when I was just trying  to knock out some simple side quests  along with everything else you will also  lose your companion information in the  bottom right corner this tracks how many  arrows of Trace can fire and the  cooldown on his ability fortunately I  don't think you really need this  information if you press square and he  doesn't fire off an arrow then you just  need to give him a few seconds typically  atreus's ability takes a long time to  recharge so I just use that once per  fight and leave it at that  everything  biggest downside of immersive mode is  that text pop-ups don't appear even if  you swipe to reveal the HUD this means  that you probably won't know what Kratos  picks up unless you check the menu with  hack silver and crafting components this  isn't as much of an issue I just assume  that it will help me craft whenever I  return to Brock and syndrome but it also  means you won't get any details on the  Collectibles you pick up during your  adventure you can always check that  stuff out in the menu but I do wish I  could at least know what some of the  stuff I'm finding is called okay so  that's how you play God of War Ragnarok  with immersive mode turned on let's talk  about the why obviously you can and  should play Ragnarok any way you see fit  it is an excellent game no matter how  you play it and this time around there's  a whole Suite of accessibility options  at your disposal go wild especially in  the early hours however immersive mode  really stands out because it forces you  to find a rhythm to combat on your own  without a HUD to rely on this means  you'll have to pay attention to your  surroundings focus on the Fantastic  sound design and work together with your  companions to a higher degree sure the  game isn't perfect for completely  hudless playthroughs but it's pretty  damn close the world Sony Santa Monica  has created is Rich with detail and all  too easy to get lost in playing with  immersive mode just removes one more  barrier from that experience anyway let  me know what you think do you plan on  trying immersive mode or will you stick  to the default settings for more on God  of War Ragnarok stay tuned to GameSpot
 

